<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hipponax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hipponax of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> was a Greek iambic poet of the sixth century BCE who was expelled from Ephesus by its tyrants and lived at Clazomenae. His abusive, comic verse about beggars, thieves and his enemies, written in a mixed Ionic dialect, used the limping iambic metre (choliambic) that he is credited with inventing. Only fragments of his work survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hipponax.jpg" alt="Hipponax" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille(1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hipponax_of_Ephesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ancient authorities record only the barest details of the life of Hipponax, sometimes contradicting each other. He is dated to 541 or 540 BCE, a date supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, who places the sculptors Bupalus and Athenis, his targets, in his lifetime. The Byzantine encyclopaedia Suda records that he was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> by the tyrants Athenagoras and Comas and settled in Clazomenae. According to Athenaeus he was small, thin and surprisingly strong, and tradition made him ugly. One scholiast reports that he sought to marry the daughter of Bupalus and was rejected, and that when Bupalus portrayed him as ugly Hipponax retaliated in verse so savage that Bupalus hanged himself. The story resembles that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a>, which invites scepticism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hipponax composed within the iambic tradition, which in the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a> a century earlier seems to have been ritualised abuse tied to the cults of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and in his day served for entertainment. Unlike Archilochus he often names himself, and his world is sordid and unheroic. He was regarded as the inventor of the scazon, or choliamb, the halting iambic, which replaces the last iambus of the senarius with a spondee or trochee. Ancient scholars credited him with inventing parody. Except in parody he wrote as if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> had never existed, and his Ionic Greek contains an unusually high proportion of Anatolian, particularly Lydian, loanwords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Alexandrian scholars collected his poems in two or three books, and they influenced poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> and Herodas. Aristophanes borrowed from him, and in Frogs Dionysus exclaims, Wonderful poet, Hipponax! Few fragments survived the Byzantine period: the Christian fathers disapproved of his abusive verse, and the emperor Julian forbade initiates to read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a> or Hipponax. His Ionic dialect and foreign words also made him unsuited to an education built on Attic. The longest surviving run of consecutive verses is only six lines, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> published in 1941 has parts of over fifty choliambics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hipponax” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ephesus was also a home of Greek poetry and thought: the iambic poet <b>Hipponax</b> worked there in the sixth century BCE, the philosopher Heraclitus wrote his book of sayings around 500 BCE and reportedly deposited it in the temple, and the painter Parrhasius was born in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Klazomenai, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.94,27.34&amp;year=-535" aria-label="Ephesus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5232 800 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hipponax"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 1070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 1070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.94,27.34&amp;year=-535">What was here in 535 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
